// Chapter 9: disaster recovery with asynchronous replication across two sites.
// The four v1alpha1 replication kinds as the code implements them today: the
// pair that names the two clusters, the policy that sets the cadence, the slot
// the operator creates per claim, and the one-shot ReplicationOps that fails
// over, fails back, and migrates. The chapter says in its first section that
// these kinds are being redesigned against the CSI Addons specification, and
// it keeps the two neighbors, StretchedCluster and TestFailover, to one short
// section each so that they are not mistaken for this.
TRAINING.register({
  id: 'async-dr',
  title: 'Disaster recovery with asynchronous replication across two sites',
  summary: 'Snapshot replication from one storage cluster to a second one on the same control plane: the pair, the policy, the slot per claim, and the ReplicationOps that fails over, fails back, forecasts, and migrates.',
  goal: 'after the chapter the reader can enroll the claims of a cluster into a replication policy toward a second site, read a slot\'s state, write the failover and the failback as the operations they are, and say what the API promises about recovery points and what it does not.',

  sections: [
    {
      heading: 'What asynchronous DR means here, and what will change',
      body: [
        'Asynchronous replication in simplyblock is snapshot shipping: on a cadence, a snapshot of each enrolled volume is taken on the source cluster and sent to a second cluster, which keeps a standby copy. The second cluster is a second **StorageCluster**, usually at a second site, and the two are served by **one control plane**. That is the first fact that shapes everything below: a replication relationship names two clusters as the control plane knows them, a StorageCluster name or a backend UUID, and there is no reference to a second ControlPlane anywhere in the API. The commissioned framing of this chapter said "two sites," and in the code a site is a cluster that the same control plane addresses.',
        'The kinds are the `v1alpha1` family: `ReplicationPair`, `ReplicationPolicy`, `ReplicationSlot`, and `ReplicationOps`. They are older than the `v1alpha2` conventions of the rest of the API, and the CRD redesign leaves them out of every inventory on purpose, because the subsystem is being redesigned against the CSI Addons specification, whose `VolumeReplication` kind carries the per-volume contract a DR orchestrator understands. **The shape of these four kinds will change.** The chapter teaches what is implemented today, and its sixth section says where the API is going.',
        { table: { head: ['Kind', 'Who writes it', 'What it is'], rows: [
          ['`ReplicationPair`', 'the administrator', 'the source cluster and the target cluster, reusable by several policies'],
          ['`ReplicationPolicy`', 'the administrator', 'a pair plus a cadence, a retention, and a mode, `failover` or `migration`'],
          ['`ReplicationSlot`', 'the operator', 'one per enrolled claim, the volume\'s live replication state'],
          ['`ReplicationOps`', 'a person, when it is time', 'one failover, failback, migration, trigger, or snapshot, with its outcome'],
        ] } },
        'Two more facts follow the first. **There is no recovery-point objective field** on any of the four kinds. A policy states an interval, a slot reports `status.lastReplicatedAt`, and a migration reports a forecast, and that is the whole surface. A gap analysis in the design directory, written in September 2026 against the Ramen DR stack, calls this surface thin, and nothing in the code has widened it since. **Failover is never automatic, and it happens per volume.** No controller watches the source cluster and decides that it is lost. A person writes a `ReplicationOps`, the operator makes one call to the control plane, and the control plane answers per volume, which is what `status.results` records. An operation at policy or target scope is that loop over every volume of the policy or pair, not a group failover to one consistent point. The `snapshot` action does take one crash-consistent generation of a policy\'s consistency group, but the failover does not consume it.',
        { callout: 'What the control plane does with a shipped snapshot, how the standby copy is held on the target, and what exactly is lost in an unplanned failover are control-plane internals the repository does not show. The chapter describes what the operator sends and what it reads back.', kind: 'note' },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'The pair and the policy',
      body: [
        'A `ReplicationPair` is written first. `spec.sourceCluster` names the local StorageCluster, and `spec.targetCluster` names the target by a StorageCluster name in the same namespace or by the backend UUID of the cluster. The operator resolves both through the same helper that every cluster-scoped call uses: a name is looked up among the StorageClusters of the namespace and its `status.uuid` is taken, and a value that already is a UUID is passed through unresolved, because a cluster whose StorageCluster object lives on another Kubernetes cluster cannot be found here and the control plane addresses it by that UUID anyway. A cluster that is an aggregate, a supercluster, is refused as either side. `targetCluster` is immutable.',
        'With both UUIDs the pair reconciler lists the backend\'s replication targets of the source cluster, reuses one that already points at the target cluster, or creates one, and records its id in `status.backendTargetID` with `status.ready: true`. It keeps a finalizer, and deleting the pair is held back while any policy still references it, after which the backend target is deleted.',
        'A `ReplicationPolicy` names the pair in `spec.pairRef` and adds the three things a schedule needs. `interval` is a duration such as `5m` or `1h`, which the operator rounds to whole minutes, at least one, before it is sent. `snapshotRetention` is the least number of snapshots kept on the target, at least two and three by default. `mode` is `failover`, the DR standby, or `migration`, a planned online move, and a policy in one mode is not used for the other action: a cluster-scoped migration refuses a policy that is not in `migration` mode. The policy reconciler waits for its pair to be ready, creates or reuses the backend policy under the pair\'s target, and records `status.backendPolicyID`, `status.ready`, and `status.slotCount`. Deleting a policy is held back while any slot still follows it.',
        { code: 'kubectl get relpair,repl -n simplyblock', title: 'The pair and its policies' },
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The pair', file: 'examples/async-dr/01-replicationpair.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'sourceCluster: site-a', text: 'A StorageCluster of this namespace, which the pair, the policies, and the operations all live in. Its `status.uuid` is what the control plane is called with.' },
          { at: 'targetCluster:', text: 'A UUID is passed through as it is. A name would be resolved like the source, which needs a StorageCluster of that name here. Immutable once created.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The policy', file: 'examples/async-dr/02-replicationpolicy.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'pairRef', text: 'The pair in the same namespace. Several policies may share one pair with different cadences.' },
          { at: 'mode: failover', text: 'The target is a standby. `migration` is the other mode, for the planned move the fifth section shows.' },
          { at: 'interval: 5m', text: 'The cadence, as a duration. The default. It is sent to the control plane as whole minutes.' },
          { at: 'snapshotRetention: 3', text: 'Snapshots kept on the target, at least two. The default is three.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Enrolling volumes: the annotation and the slot',
      body: [
        'Nobody writes a `ReplicationSlot`. A claim is enrolled by the annotation `storage.simplyblock.io/replication-policy`, on the claim itself or on its StorageClass, and the claim\'s annotation wins when both are set. The value is the policy\'s name, or `<namespace>/<name>` when the policy lives in another namespace than the claim, which it usually does: the policy sits with the StorageCluster in the storage namespace and the claims sit with their workloads. A watcher reconciles every claim and every StorageClass, and once an annotated claim is `Bound` it reads the volume handle from the claim\'s PersistentVolume and creates the slot, named `<policy>-<claim>`, in the claim\'s namespace, with the claim as its owner. A claim that is not yet bound is checked again every 15 seconds, and a policy that is not yet ready every 10.',
        'The slot carries three immutable fields, `policyRef`, `pvcRef`, and `volumeID`, the last being the handle `<cluster>:<pool>:<volume>`. Its reconciler attaches the volume to the backend policy with one `PUT` on the volume and sets `status.state: replicating` with `status.direction: source` at once, with the event `Replicating`. From then on it polls the backend every 15 seconds, follows the backend into the states a cutover or a failover produces, and refreshes `status.lastReplicatedAt` from the backend\'s last snapshot time, but only when the backend has a relationship record for the volume, which during ordinary replication it does not and answers with a 404, so the timestamp may stand at the attach time until something else happens.',
        { table: { head: ['`status.state`', 'When', 'What the reconciler does'], rows: [
          ['`attaching`', 'a legacy state', 'advances to `replicating` at once'],
          ['`replicating`', 'the steady state', 'polls the backend every 15 s and refreshes `lastReplicatedAt`'],
          ['`cutover_pending`', 'a migration commit was accepted', 'runs the preconnect Job on the consuming nodes and signals the backend to proceed'],
          ['`cutover_done`', 'the volume has moved', '`direction: target`, the volume is served from the target cluster'],
          ['`failed_over`', 'after an unplanned failover', '`direction: target`, `targetNQN` set, event `FailedOver`'],
          ['`detaching`', 'the annotation was removed or the claim deleted', 'detaches the volume with `replication_policy_id: null`, then drops its finalizer'],
          ['`error`', 'a backend call failed', 'retries the attach'],
        ] } },
        'Changing the annotation of a bound claim to another policy deletes the slot, waits for the detach, and creates a new slot against the new policy, which is a full copy on the backend. Removing the annotation detaches. Deleting the claim garbage-collects the slot, whose finalizer detaches the volume before the object goes.',
        { code: 'kubectl get relslot -A\nkubectl get events -n shop --field-selector involvedObject.kind=ReplicationSlot', title: 'The slots and their events' },
        { pitfalls: [
          { symptom: 'The pair never reports `ready`, and its message names the cluster.', cause: '`sourceCluster` or `targetCluster` is a name no StorageCluster of the namespace carries, the cluster has no `status.uuid` yet, or it is a supercluster.', fix: 'Name a StorageCluster of the pair\'s namespace that is `Online`, or write the target as its backend UUID when its object lives on another Kubernetes cluster.' },
          { symptom: 'An annotated claim gets no slot.', cause: 'The claim is not `Bound`, the policy named by the annotation is not found in the namespace the reference resolves to, or the policy is not ready yet.', fix: 'Write the reference as `<namespace>/<name>` when the policy is not in the claim\'s namespace, and wait for `status.ready` on the policy. The watcher retries on its own.' },
          { symptom: 'A slot is in `error` with `attach failed`.', cause: 'The control plane refused the `PUT` that attaches the policy, which the design says it does while a cutover of the volume is in flight.', fix: 'Read the slot\'s `status.message`. The reconciler retries the attach from `error` on its own.' },
          { symptom: 'The policy cannot be deleted.', cause: 'Slots still follow it, in any namespace, and its finalizer waits for them.', fix: 'Remove the annotation from the claims or delete them, and the policy goes once the last slot has detached. The pair, in turn, waits for its policies.' },
        ] },
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'A class that enrolls its claims', file: 'examples/async-dr/03-storageclass.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'storage.simplyblock.io/replication-policy', text: 'The annotation the watcher reads, on a class or a claim. On a class it applies to every claim bound through it. The policy is in the `simplyblock` namespace and the claims are not, so the reference is qualified, and a bare name would mean the claim\'s own namespace.' },
          { at: 'cluster_id', text: 'With `pool_name`, what the driver needs to reach the backend. A hand-written class carries the same two parameters the operator writes on the cluster\'s default class.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'A claim that enrolls itself', file: 'examples/async-dr/04-pvc.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'replication-policy: simplyblock/dr-policy', text: 'On a claim, the annotation wins over its class. It can be set at creation or added, changed, or removed after the claim is bound.' },
          { at: 'storageClassName: simplyblock-site-a', text: 'The cluster\'s plain default class. The policy comes from the claim\'s own annotation, not from the class.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Unplanned failover, the failback, and the lock',
      body: [
        'A `ReplicationOps` has an `action` and a `scope`, both immutable, and a `ref` that names what the scope says: a pair for `target`, a policy for `policy`, a slot for `volume`, and a StorageCluster for `cluster`, which only a migration takes. An admission webhook refuses an operation whose `ref` does not name an object of that kind in the operation\'s namespace, so a typo is caught before anything runs. The operation lives in the policy\'s namespace, because that is where the reconciler looks the policy up.',
        'The **failover** is the unplanned one: the source may be down, and the target\'s last replicated copy is promoted. At policy scope the reconciler resolves the pair and the source cluster, collects the policy\'s slots, sets `status.subphase: TriggeringFailover`, and posts one failover to the backend policy. The backend answers 200 with one entry per volume, and an entry marked failed fails the whole operation with the volume and its detail in `status.message`. Otherwise, the subphase moves to `UpdatingSlotStatuses`, every collected slot is set to `failed_over` with `direction: target` and the target volume id, `status.results` lists each slot as `succeeded`, the event `FailoverSucceeded` is recorded, and the phase is `Succeeded`. Target scope does the same for every policy of a pair through the backend target, and volume scope posts the failover of one volume.',
        'The operation collects the slots of its own namespace. A slot beside a claim in another namespace, which is the usual case, is not listed in `status.results` of the operation, and it reports the failover itself within its 15 second poll, from the backend\'s state, with `targetNQN` filled in and the event `FailedOver`. A pod that is staged again afterward is connected to the volume on the target cluster, which the CSI node plugin resolves from the backend\'s relationship record rather than from the volume handle it was given.',
        'The **failback** is the way home, in two steps per slot that the operator drives: a failback call on the target cluster, which syncs the final snapshot back to the source, and a commit, which the backend accepts with 202 and completes on its own by switching the active path back. The subphases are `StartingFailback`, `CommittingFailback`, and then `WaitingForSlots`, during which each slot is `cutover_pending` and the operation polls every 30 seconds until every slot is `replicating` with `direction: source` again, which is when it reports `Succeeded` with the event `FailbackComplete`. `spec.sourceClusterID` names the cluster to recover to, as the control plane knows it, and is omitted to recover to the original source. `spec.deleteSource` deletes the failed-over volume on the target after the commit. Failback takes scope `policy` or `volume`, and scope `target` is refused with a message that says so.',
        'One operation at a time holds the **lock**: `ReplicationPolicy.status.activeOpsRef` for every scope but `target`, and `ReplicationPair.status.activeOpsRef` for that one. A second operation on the same policy or pair stays in `Pending` and is checked again every 10 seconds until the first has ended, and the lock is released when the operation reaches `Succeeded` or `Failed`, or is deleted. The shape of this kind is older than the `v1alpha2` operations pattern: `status.phase` is a string with four values, `status.subphase` is free text, and there is no abort. Deleting a running operation releases its lock and nothing else, so a migration plan already created keeps running in the control plane.',
        { code: 'kubectl get replops -n simplyblock -w\nkubectl get repl dr-policy -n simplyblock -o jsonpath=\'{.status.activeOpsRef}\'', title: 'Following an operation and its lock' },
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The failover', file: 'examples/async-dr/05-replicationops-failover.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'action: failover', text: 'Unplanned. The control plane promotes the last replicated snapshot on the target, and the writes since it are lost.' },
          { at: 'scope: policy', text: 'Every slot of the policy, as one backend call that answers per volume. `target` takes every policy of a pair, `volume` one slot.' },
          { at: 'ref: dr-policy', text: 'The policy\'s name, in the operation\'s namespace. Admission refuses a name that does not resolve.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The failback', file: 'examples/async-dr/06-replicationops-failback.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'action: failback', text: 'Two backend calls per slot, then a wait until every slot is `replicating` toward the target again. `Succeeded` is the end of that wait.' },
          { at: 'sourceClusterID: 7c2e', text: 'The cluster to recover to, as the control plane knows it. Omitted, the original source. Used by `failback` only.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Planned migration, the dry run, and replicating now',
      body: [
        'A **migration** is the planned move with both clusters up: the volumes are copied, then cut over one at a time per source volume store so that each I/O freeze stays short, and the paths switch to the target cluster without the workload losing its volume. It is the only action that runs for hours. The reconciler enrolls what is not replicated yet, waits for every slot to be `replicating`, creates one migration plan in the control plane with every volume, and then reads the plan back every 30 seconds, mirroring each volume\'s state, target node, bytes remaining, and ETA into `status.results` and the plan\'s forecast into `status.forecast`. The subphases are `Enrolling`, `AwaitingReplication`, `CreatingPlan`, and `TrackingPlan`, and the plan\'s id is kept in `status.planID` so that a restart adopts the plan rather than creating a second one. A plan that is `done` ends the operation `Succeeded`, with the event `MigrationComplete`, and one that is `partial`, `failed`, or `canceled` ends it `Failed` with the results kept.',
        'The forecast is the only estimate of duration the API has. It carries `bytesRemaining`, `etaSeconds`, `completesAt`, the throughput assumed and whether it was measured, and `converging`, which is false when some source volume store is written at least as fast as it replicates, so that its lag never closes and no cutover can start. The event `NotConverging` names the stores, and `status.warnings` lists the risks the control plane found when it planned, such as too little free capacity on the source for the snapshots the copy pins.',
        'Scope `cluster` is the migration\'s own: `ref` names the source StorageCluster, and `spec.policyRef` names a policy in `migration` mode under which every bound claim of the cluster, in every namespace, that is not replicated yet is enrolled by setting its annotation. A claim that already follows another policy is skipped, never re-enrolled. **`spec.dryRun`** asks for the plan and the forecast only: nothing is enrolled, replicated, or cut over, the operation succeeds once the forecast is in its status, with the event `MigrationForecast`, and against a control plane that has no migration plans it fails, because nothing can forecast.',
        'Two smaller actions share the kind. **`trigger`** takes a snapshot of each volume of its scope now and replicates it, outside the policy\'s interval. Only a `replicating` slot is triggered: a `failed_over` or `cutover_done` slot is `skipped` with the reason, a slot in `error` is `failed`, and each outcome is written to `status.results` as it happens, so a reconcile that resumes does not trigger a slot twice. **`snapshot`** takes one crash-consistent generation of a policy\'s consistency group, which is why it takes scope `policy` or `target` only, and the CRD refuses scope `volume` for it. A policy without a consistency group fails the operation at policy scope and is skipped at target scope.',
        { pitfalls: [
          { symptom: 'The operation is refused at admission.', cause: '`ref` names nothing of the scope\'s kind in the namespace, `policyRef` is set without scope `cluster` or missing with it, `dryRun` is set on an action other than `migration`, or the policy named by `policyRef` is not in `migration` mode.', fix: 'Read the message. Each rule names the field and what it expects.' },
          { symptom: 'A second operation stays in `Pending`.', cause: 'Another operation holds `activeOpsRef` on the policy or the pair.', fix: 'Wait for it to end, or delete it, which releases the lock and leaves any migration plan running in the control plane.' },
          { symptom: 'A `trigger` reports every volume `skipped`, with `fail it back first`.', cause: 'The slots are `failed_over`. The source the trigger would snapshot is the superseded one.', fix: 'Fail back first. A trigger only snapshots a `replicating` slot.' },
          { symptom: 'A migration sits in `TrackingPlan` with `converging: false`.', cause: 'Some source volume store is written faster than it replicates, so the delta never shrinks and no cutover starts.', fix: 'Reduce the writes to the stores the `NotConverging` event names, or move fewer volumes at once. The plan continues meanwhile.' },
          { symptom: 'A dry run fails with `no migration plans`.', cause: 'The control plane predates migration plans. A real migration on it falls back to one commit per slot, but nothing can forecast.', fix: 'Run the migration without `dryRun`, or upgrade the control plane.' },
        ] },
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'A migration policy and a dry run', file: 'examples/async-dr/07-replicationops-migration-dryrun.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'mode: migration', text: 'A cluster-scoped migration enrolls claims under a policy in this mode only. The `failover` policy of the DR standby is not used for a move.' },
          { at: 'scope: cluster', text: 'Every bound claim on the source cluster, in every namespace. Allowed with `action: migration` only.' },
          { at: 'ref: site-a', text: 'The source StorageCluster, by name or UUID. It must be the cluster the policy\'s pair replicates from.' },
          { at: 'policyRef: move-policy', text: 'Required with scope `cluster` and refused elsewhere. Claims that are not replicated yet are annotated with it.' },
          { at: 'dryRun: true', text: 'The plan and its forecast only. Nothing is enrolled, replicated, or cut over, and the operation succeeds once `status.forecast` is written.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Where the API is going: VolumeReplication through CSI Addons',
      body: [
        'The CSI driver already serves the CSI Addons Replication service, as an adapter over the same control-plane calls: `EnableVolumeReplication` attaches a volume to a backend policy, `DisableVolumeReplication` detaches it, `GetVolumeReplicationInfo` reports the last sync time, `PromoteVolume` is the failover, planned or forced, `DemoteVolume` fences the source and takes its final snapshot, and `ResyncVolume` is the failback. The user-facing kinds on that path are the upstream `VolumeReplication` and `VolumeReplicationClass`, reconciled by the kubernetes-csi-addons controller manager, which the chart deploys and the `SimplyblockDriver` adds the sidecar for when `spec.enableCSIAddons` is set. Phases 1 to 3 of that design are implemented, and group replication is planned.',
        'The `ReplicationPair` and the `ReplicationPolicy` keep their job on that path too: they author the backend target and policy, and a `VolumeReplicationClass` names the policy to attach in its parameters. The parameter the code reads is `replicationPolicyID`, the backend policy\'s id, where the design document writes `replicationPolicy`, a name, because resolving a name is a call the shared library does not wrap yet. The design\'s rule is one owner per volume: a volume is managed by a slot, through the annotation, or by a `VolumeReplication`, never both, and moving a volume between the two paths is done by removing the annotation and letting the slot detach before the `VolumeReplication` is written. The code does not check one path against the other today, so keeping them apart is the user\'s discipline.',
        'What this changes for the reader of this chapter: the slot and the imperative `ReplicationOps` become the compatibility layer, and the declarative per-volume `replicationState`, `primary` or `secondary`, becomes the way a DR orchestrator drives simplyblock. Until that lands, this chapter is how it is done.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'StretchedCluster is the synchronous cousin',
      body: [
        'A `StretchedCluster` is not this chapter. It is one backend cluster whose two sites are two Kubernetes clusters, replicated **synchronously**, requested from an Open Cluster Management hub that also runs the control plane. Its spec names the two managed clusters, the backend site each represents, and which one leads: the leader\'s operator creates the backend cluster and runs every cluster-wide operation, the follower adopts it and manages its own nodes. The hub carries the request to both sites as ManifestWork and reads their answers back through views, and the phases run `Pending`, `Discovering`, `Drafted`, `LeaderCreating`, `FollowerJoining`, `Ready`, with `Degraded` and `Failed` beside them.',
        'Its controller is hub-only: it watches ManifestWork, which only the hub serves, so the operator registers it where that API exists and otherwise keeps checking and starts it once OCM is installed. There is no pair, no policy, no slot, and no failover operation in it, because the data is on both sites at once and a `VolumeReplicationGroup` turning primary is what moves the leadership of a volume store, through a follower controller of its own.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'TestFailover is a drill, not a failover',
      body: [
        'A `TestFailover` is not this chapter either. It is a `v1alpha2` kind for a **non-disruptive drill**: a source, a PVC for scope `Volume` or a consistency group for scope `Group`, on an OCM managed cluster, is recovered as a clone onto a different managed cluster, the bubble cluster, in a namespace there, and torn down on deletion or after `ttlSeconds`. The source keeps running throughout, nothing is promoted, and no slot changes state. Its phases are `Pending`, `Provisioning`, `Ready`, `Failed`, and `TearingDown`.',
        'Like the StretchedCluster, its controller runs on the hub only, because it reads the source through a ManagedClusterView and places the clone through ManifestWork. A drill is how the recovery is rehearsed. The failover of this chapter is the thing itself.',
      ],
    },
  ],

  questions: [
    { topic: 'The model', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are true of asynchronous replication as the operator implements it?', options: ['Both clusters are served by one control plane', 'A ReplicationPair references a second ControlPlane', 'The target cluster can be given as a backend UUID', 'The source cluster is the name of a local StorageCluster', 'Replication runs continuously at the block level'], correct: [0, 2, 3], explain: 'One control plane addresses both clusters by UUID, and the pair names the source as a local StorageCluster and the target as a name or a UUID. There is no reference to a second ControlPlane, and the mechanism is snapshot shipping on a cadence.' },
    { topic: 'The model', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which fields of the four replication kinds state a recovery-point objective?', options: ['`ReplicationPolicy.spec.rpo`', '`ReplicationPair.spec.rpoSeconds`', '`ReplicationSlot.spec.recoveryPoint`', '`ReplicationOps.spec.rpo`', '`ReplicationPolicy.spec.interval`'], correct: [], explain: 'None of them exists. The interval is a cadence, not an objective, and the only signals are `ReplicationSlot.status.lastReplicatedAt` and a migration\'s forecast. The gap analysis calls that surface thin.' },
    { topic: 'The model', cat: 'concept', text: 'When does a failover happen?', options: ['When the operator detects that the source cluster is unreachable', 'When a person creates a ReplicationOps with `action: failover`', 'When a slot\'s `lastReplicatedAt` grows older than the interval', 'When the control plane reports the source cluster as down', 'When the ReplicationPair is deleted'], correct: [1], explain: 'Failover is never automatic. Nothing in the operator watches the source and decides, and the pair\'s deletion tears down the backend target rather than promoting anything.' },
    { topic: 'The model', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about a failover at policy scope are true?', options: ['The control plane answers per volume, and `status.results` records each', 'One entry marked failed fails the whole operation', 'The volumes are failed over to one consistency-group generation', 'The slots of the policy are set to `failed_over`', 'The operation is retried automatically until every volume succeeds'], correct: [0, 1, 3], explain: 'A policy-scope failover is one backend call that returns one entry per volume, a failed entry fails the operation, and the collected slots are stamped `failed_over`. It does not consume a group generation, and a Failed operation is not retried.' },
    { topic: 'The model', cat: 'concept', text: 'Why does the CRD redesign leave the four replication kinds out of its inventories?', options: ['They are deprecated and have no controllers', 'The subsystem is being redesigned against the CSI Addons specification', 'They belong to the CSI driver rather than the operator', 'They are cluster-scoped and the redesign covers namespaced kinds only', 'They are generated from the control plane\'s OpenAPI'], correct: [1], explain: 'The redesign says so explicitly: `VolumeReplication` and `VolumeGroupReplication` already carry the contract a DR orchestrator understands, so nothing in it settles how replication is modeled. The kinds are implemented and reconciled today.' },
    { topic: 'The model', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which API versions do the replication kinds of this chapter carry?', options: ['`storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha1`', '`storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2`', '`replication.storage.openshift.io/v1alpha1`', '`storage.simplyblock.io/v1`', '`csiaddons.openshift.io/v1alpha1`'], correct: [0], explain: 'All four are `v1alpha1`, older than the rest of the API. The `replication.storage.openshift.io` group is the upstream CSI Addons family the API is moving toward.' },
    { topic: 'Pair and policy', cat: 'field', text: 'Which values does `ReplicationPair.spec.targetCluster` accept?', options: ['The name of a StorageCluster in the pair\'s namespace', 'The backend UUID of the target cluster', 'The name of a ControlPlane', 'A Kubernetes cluster\'s API server URL', 'The name of a supercluster'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'A name is resolved to the StorageCluster\'s `status.uuid`, and a UUID is passed through for a cluster whose object lives elsewhere. A supercluster is refused as either side, and there is no ControlPlane or API server reference.' },
    { topic: 'Pair and policy', cat: 'concept', text: 'What does the ReplicationPair reconciler do once both cluster UUIDs resolve?', options: ['Lists the source cluster\'s backend replication targets and reuses one pointing at the target cluster', 'Creates a backend replication target when none exists', 'Records the target\'s id in `status.backendTargetID`', 'Creates one ReplicationSlot per volume of the source cluster', 'Starts the first replication snapshot'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The pair owns the backend target and nothing else. Slots come from annotated claims, and snapshots from the policy\'s cadence on the backend.' },
    { topic: 'Pair and policy', cat: 'field', text: 'Which values of `ReplicationPolicy.spec.interval` are accepted and how are they sent?', options: ['A duration such as `5m` or `1h`, sent as whole minutes', 'A cron expression', 'A number of seconds, sent as is', '`30s`, rounded up to one minute', 'An RFC 3339 timestamp of the first snapshot'], correct: [0, 3], explain: 'The operator parses a Go duration and rounds it to whole minutes, with one as the floor, before the backend policy is created. The default is `5m`.' },
    { topic: 'Pair and policy', cat: 'field', text: 'What is the smallest `snapshotRetention` a ReplicationPolicy accepts, and the default?', options: ['Smallest 1, default 1', 'Smallest 2, default 3', 'Smallest 0, default 5', 'Smallest 3, default 3', 'There is no minimum and no default'], correct: [1], explain: 'The CRD sets a minimum of two and a default of three. It is the least number of snapshots kept on the target.' },
    { topic: 'Pair and policy', cat: 'field', text: 'Which of these are modes of a ReplicationPolicy?', options: ['`failover`', '`migration`', '`synchronous`', '`backup`', '`active-active`'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Two modes: the DR standby and the planned move. Synchronous replication is the StretchedCluster\'s business, and backups are another chapter.' },
    { topic: 'Pair and policy', cat: 'concept', text: 'A ReplicationPair is deleted while two policies still reference it. What happens?', options: ['The pair\'s finalizer holds it until the policies are gone', 'The policies are deleted with it', 'The backend target is deleted at once and the policies go to `error`', 'The deletion is refused at admission', 'The policies are re-pointed at another pair'], correct: [0], explain: 'Deletion ordering mirrors creation: the pair waits for its policies, and a policy waits for its slots. The backend target is deleted only once the pair is clear.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'field', text: 'Where can the annotation `storage.simplyblock.io/replication-policy` be set, and which wins?', options: ['On a PersistentVolumeClaim', 'On a StorageClass', 'On a StoragePool', 'On the ReplicationPair', 'The claim\'s annotation wins over the class\'s'], correct: [0, 1, 4], explain: 'The watcher reads claims and StorageClasses, and resolves the claim\'s annotation first. Pools and pairs carry no such annotation.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'field', text: 'A policy `dr-policy` lives in `simplyblock` and a claim lives in `shop`. What does the claim\'s annotation say?', options: ['`dr-policy`', '`simplyblock/dr-policy`', '`dr-policy.simplyblock`', '`simplyblock:dr-policy`', 'A policy cannot be referenced across namespaces'], correct: [1], explain: 'A bare name means the claim\'s own namespace, and `<namespace>/<name>` names a policy elsewhere. The watcher, the slot reconciler, and the policy reconciler all resolve both forms.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are true of a ReplicationSlot?', options: ['It is created by the operator, never by hand', 'It is named `<policy>-<claim>`', 'It lives in the policy\'s namespace', 'It is owned by its claim', 'It is created as soon as the claim exists, bound or not'], correct: [0, 1, 3], explain: 'The watcher creates the slot beside the claim once the claim is `Bound`, with the claim as owner, so the slot lives in the claim\'s namespace rather than the policy\'s.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'field', text: 'Which states can `ReplicationSlot.status.state` hold?', options: ['`replicating`', '`cutover_pending`', '`failed_over`', '`detaching`', '`error`'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], explain: 'All five, with `attaching` and `cutover_done` beside them. The enum on the status field lists exactly these seven.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about `status.lastReplicatedAt` are true?', options: ['It is set to the attach time when the slot starts replicating', 'It is refreshed from the backend only when the backend has a relationship record for the volume', 'It is the only per-volume timing signal the API has', 'It is a lag figure in seconds', 'It is refreshed on every one of the reconciler\'s 15 second polls'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The attach writes the current time, and the steady-state poll refreshes it from the backend\'s last snapshot time only when the backend answers with a record, which during ordinary replication it does not. There is no lag figure.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'action', text: 'The annotation of a bound claim is changed from one policy to another. What does the operator do?', options: ['Deletes the slot, waits for the detach, and creates a new slot against the new policy', 'Edits `spec.policyRef` on the slot', 'Refuses the change at admission', 'Keeps both slots until the first finishes', 'Nothing until the claim is re-bound'], correct: [0], explain: 'The slot\'s spec is immutable, so a policy change is a detach and a re-attach, which is a full copy on the backend.' },
    { topic: 'Slots', cat: 'concept', text: 'What happens to a slot when its claim is deleted?', options: ['The slot is garbage-collected through its owner reference', 'The slot\'s finalizer detaches the volume from the policy before the object goes', 'The slot stays as a record of the replication', 'The policy is deleted with it', 'The target cluster\'s copy is promoted'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Owner reference and finalizer together: the deletion cascades, and the detach runs first. The policy and the target copy are untouched.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'field', text: 'Which `ref` does a ReplicationOps carry for each scope?', options: ['A ReplicationPair name for `target`', 'A ReplicationPolicy name for `policy`', 'A ReplicationSlot name for `volume`', 'A StorageCluster name or UUID for `cluster`', 'A PersistentVolumeClaim name for `volume`'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'Each scope names its own kind, and the admission webhook refuses a `ref` that does not resolve to one. A volume is named by its slot, not its claim.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'action', text: 'A failover at policy scope has reached `Succeeded`. Which of these are now true?', options: ['Every slot collected by the operation is `failed_over` with `direction: target`', '`status.results` lists each collected slot as `succeeded`', 'The event `FailoverSucceeded` was recorded on the operation', 'The policy\'s `activeOpsRef` is empty again', 'The source cluster\'s StorageCluster has been deleted'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The operation stamps the slots, records the results and the event, and releases the lock as it succeeds. Nothing happens to the StorageCluster objects.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'concept', text: 'A slot beside a claim in the `shop` namespace follows a policy in `simplyblock`, and a policy-scope failover runs in `simplyblock`. What is true of that slot?', options: ['It is not listed in the operation\'s `status.results`', 'Its own reconciler reports `failed_over` from the backend within its poll', 'It is left `replicating` for good', 'The failover refuses to run because of it', 'It is moved to the `simplyblock` namespace'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The operation collects the slots of its own namespace only, and the slot\'s reconciler follows the backend into `failed_over`, with `targetNQN` and the event `FailedOver`, on its 15 second poll.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'action', text: 'Which steps does the failback perform for each slot?', options: ['A failback call on the target cluster, syncing the final snapshot back', 'A commit, accepted with 202 and completed by the backend', 'A wait until the slot is `replicating` with `direction: source`', 'A deletion and re-creation of the claim', 'A reboot of the source cluster\'s storage nodes'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'Two calls and a wait. `Succeeded` appears only once every slot is home, which is what the `WaitingForSlots` subphase polls for every 30 seconds.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'field', text: 'Which ReplicationOps fields apply to a failback?', options: ['`spec.sourceClusterID`', '`spec.deleteSource`', '`spec.dryRun`', '`spec.policyRef`', '`spec.interval`'], correct: [0, 1], explain: '`sourceClusterID` names the cluster to recover to and `deleteSource` removes the failed-over volume on the target after the commit. `dryRun` is migration only, `policyRef` is cluster scope only, and there is no interval on an operation.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which scopes does a failback accept?', options: ['`policy`', '`volume`', '`target`', '`cluster`', '`pair`'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Scope `target` is refused with a message pointing at `policy`, `cluster` belongs to migration, and `pair` is not a scope.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about the operation lock are true?', options: ['It is `ReplicationPolicy.status.activeOpsRef` for every scope but `target`', 'It is `ReplicationPair.status.activeOpsRef` for scope `target`', 'A second operation is refused at admission while the lock is held', 'A second operation waits in `Pending` and is checked every 10 seconds', 'Deleting the holder releases the lock'], correct: [0, 1, 3, 4], explain: 'Two locks, one per policy and one per pair. A contender waits rather than being refused, and the lock is released on `Succeeded`, `Failed`, or deletion.' },
    { topic: 'Failover and failback', cat: 'concept', text: 'How does the ReplicationOps shape differ from the `v1alpha2` operations pattern?', options: ['`status.phase` is a string with four values', '`status.subphase` is free text', 'There is no abort', 'It has no `status.results`', 'It is cluster-scoped'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The kind predates the pattern: string phase, free subphase, no abort. It does have per-volume results, and it is namespaced.' },
    { topic: 'Migration', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these does a migration do that a failover does not?', options: ['Copies the data with both clusters up before any cutover', 'Cuts the volumes over one at a time per source volume store', 'Reports a forecast of bytes remaining and time to completion', 'Stays `Running` until every volume has cut over', 'Promotes the target\'s last snapshot and loses the writes since'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'A migration is the planned, online move through a migration plan. Losing the writes since the last snapshot is what an unplanned failover accepts.' },
    { topic: 'Migration', cat: 'field', text: 'Which statements about `spec.dryRun` are true?', options: ['It is allowed with `action: migration` only', 'Nothing is enrolled, replicated, or cut over', 'The operation succeeds once the forecast is in its status', 'It fails against a control plane without migration plans', 'It reserves capacity on the target for the move'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'A dry run asks the control plane for the plan and the forecast and changes nothing. Without migration plans in the control plane nothing can forecast, so it fails.' },
    { topic: 'Migration', cat: 'field', text: 'What does scope `cluster` require?', options: ['`action: migration`', '`spec.policyRef` naming a policy in `migration` mode', '`ref` naming the source StorageCluster, by name or UUID', '`spec.dryRun: true`', 'That every claim of the cluster is already replicated'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'Cluster scope is the migration\'s own, enrolls the claims that are not replicated yet under `policyRef`, and takes the source cluster as `ref`. A dry run is optional.' },
    { topic: 'Migration', cat: 'concept', text: 'What does `status.forecast.converging: false` mean?', options: ['Some source volume store is written at least as fast as it replicates, so no cutover can start', 'The control plane lost the plan', 'The target cluster is full', 'The forecast is measured rather than assumed', 'The operation has failed'], correct: [0], explain: 'The lag of such a store never closes, `etaSeconds` and `completesAt` stay unset, and the `NotConverging` event names the stores. The plan keeps running.' },
    { topic: 'Migration', cat: 'action', text: 'A `trigger` runs over a policy whose slots are all `failed_over`. What is the outcome?', options: ['Every volume is `skipped`, with a reason to fail back first', 'Every volume is snapshotted on the target cluster', 'The operation fails over the volumes again', 'The slots return to `replicating`', 'The operation is refused at admission'], correct: [0], explain: 'A trigger snapshots the pair\'s source cluster, which after a failover holds the superseded copy, so only a `replicating` slot is triggered and the rest are skipped with the reason.' },
    { topic: 'Migration', cat: 'field', text: 'Which scopes does the `snapshot` action accept?', options: ['`policy`', '`target`', '`volume`', '`cluster`', 'Any of the four'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'A generation is a consistency group\'s, which is a policy\'s, so the CRD refuses scope `volume` and the action takes `policy` or `target`.' },
    { topic: 'Neighbors', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are true of the CSI Addons path?', options: ['The driver serves the Replication gRPC as an adapter over the same control-plane calls', '`VolumeReplication` and `VolumeReplicationClass` are the user-facing kinds', 'The ReplicationPair and ReplicationPolicy still author the backend target and policy', 'The class parameter the code reads is `replicationPolicyID`', 'A volume can be managed by a slot and a VolumeReplication at the same time'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The adapter, the upstream kinds, and the authoring kinds all hold, and the parameter is the policy\'s id rather than the name the design wrote. The rule is one owner per volume.' },
    { topic: 'Neighbors', cat: 'concept', text: 'How does a StretchedCluster differ from this chapter\'s replication?', options: ['It replicates synchronously', 'Its two sites are two Kubernetes clusters managed from one OCM hub', 'Its controller runs on the hub only', 'It uses a ReplicationPolicy with a shorter interval', 'It fails over through a ReplicationOps'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'One backend cluster across two managed clusters, synchronous, requested from the hub that also runs the control plane. No pair, no policy, no slot, and no ReplicationOps are involved.' },
    { topic: 'Neighbors', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about a TestFailover are true?', options: ['It recovers a clone of the source onto a different managed cluster', 'The source keeps running throughout', 'It sets the source\'s slot to `failed_over`', 'It is a `v1alpha2` kind whose controller is hub-only', 'It is how a real failover is started'], correct: [0, 1, 3], explain: 'A drill: a clone in a bubble namespace on another cluster, torn down on deletion or after its TTL. No slot changes state, and the real failover is a ReplicationOps.' },
  ],

  tasks: [
    {
      title: 'The DR policy',
      scenario: 'Write the policy that replicates toward the standby site every ten minutes, keeps the last four snapshots there, and leaves the promotion to a ReplicationOps.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha1\nkind: {{kind}}\nmetadata:\n  name: dr-policy\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  pairRef: {{pair}}\n  mode: {{mode}}\n  interval: {{interval}}\n  snapshotRetention: {{retention}}\n',
      blanks: {
        kind: { accept: ['ReplicationPolicy'], hint: 'the kind that carries a cadence, short name `repl`' },
        pair: { accept: ['site-a-to-site-b'], hint: 'the pair of this chapter' },
        mode: { accept: ['failover'], hint: 'the standby mode, which is the default' },
        interval: { accept: ['10m', '600s', '0h10m'], hint: 'a duration, sent as whole minutes' },
        retention: { accept: ['4'], hint: 'kept on the target, at least two' },
      },
      explain: 'A `ReplicationPolicy` names its pair, and `failover` is the standby mode. The interval is a duration the operator rounds to minutes, and the retention is the count kept on the target.',
    },
    {
      title: 'The failover',
      scenario: 'Site-a is lost. Write the operation that promotes every volume under `dr-policy` on the standby site.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha1\nkind: {{kind}}\nmetadata:\n  name: failover-dr-policy\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  action: {{action}}\n  scope: {{scope}}\n  ref: {{ref}}\n',
      blanks: {
        kind: { accept: ['ReplicationOps'], hint: 'the one-shot operation kind, short name `replops`' },
        action: { accept: ['failover'], hint: 'the unplanned one' },
        scope: { accept: ['policy'], hint: 'every slot of one policy' },
        ref: { accept: ['dr-policy'], hint: 'what the scope names' },
      },
      explain: 'A `ReplicationOps` with `action: failover` at `scope: policy` posts one failover to the backend policy named by `ref`, and the control plane answers per volume.',
    },
    {
      title: 'The forecast of a move',
      scenario: 'Before moving every claim of `site-a` to the second site, ask the control plane how much data that is and how long it takes, without enrolling or copying anything.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ReplicationOps\nmetadata:\n  name: forecast-site-a-move\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  action: {{action}}\n  scope: {{scope}}\n  ref: site-a\n  policyRef: {{policy}}\n  dryRun: {{dry}}\n',
      blanks: {
        action: { accept: ['migration'], hint: 'the planned move, the only action a dry run is allowed on' },
        scope: { accept: ['cluster'], hint: 'every bound claim of a StorageCluster, in every namespace' },
        policy: { accept: ['move-policy'], hint: 'the policy in `migration` mode of this chapter' },
        dry: { accept: ['true'], hint: 'the plan and its forecast only' },
      },
      explain: 'Scope `cluster` is the migration\'s own and needs a `policyRef` in `migration` mode. `dryRun: true` publishes the plan and the forecast and changes nothing.',
    },
  ],
});
